A presidential election was held in Portugal on 14 January 2001.

Incumbent president [[Jorge Sampaio]] was eliglbe to run for a second consecutive term, and was a candidate for re-election.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2001-01-07 |title=Comissão de honra de Jorge Sampaio |url=https://arquivos.rtp.pt/conteudos/comissao-de-honra-de-jorge-sampaio/ | website= RTP Arquivos |access-date=2026-08-28 |language=pt-PT}}</ref> Sampaio was easily re-elected for a second term, like what happened with [[Mário Soares]] and [[Ramalho Eanes]], gathering 55% of the votes and winning all districts except [[Bragança District|Bragança]].

As the re-election of the center-left president was certain, both the [[Portuguese Communist Party]] and the [[Left Bloc]] (BE), the latter for the first time, presented their own candidates. The Communists presented [[António Abreu]], a candidate with low name recognition among voters,<ref>{{Cite web |date=2001-01-12 |title=O candidato António Abreu |url=https://arquivos.rtp.pt/conteudos/o-candidato-antonio-abreu/ | website= RTP Arquivos |access-date=2026-08-28 |language=pt-PT}}</ref> which resulted in poor results, just 5% of the votes, while the BE supported [[Fernando Rosas]], who polled at 3% of the votes. The [[Communist Party of the Portuguese Workers]] also presented its own candidate for the first time in its history, [[António Garcia Pereira|Garcia Pereira]], polling at 1.6% of the votes. On the right, Ferreira do Amaral was supported by the two major parties from that side, the [[Social Democratic Party (Portugal)|Social Democratic Party]] and the [[CDS – People's Party]], winning approximately 35% of the votes and polling second, failing once again the objective of electing a right-wing president for the first time since the [[Carnation Revolution]].

Turnout was quite low, just 49.71 percent of voters cast a ballot, however, this was the first presidential election in which Portuguese overseas voters were able to cast a ballot.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2001-01-12 |title=Primeiro dia de votação nas comunidades portuguesas com pouca afluência |url=https://www.publico.pt/2001/01/12/politica/noticia/primeiro-dia-de-votacao-nas-comunidades-portuguesas-com-pouca-afluencia-6852 |access-date=2026-07-09| website=Público |language=pt-PT}}</ref> Jorge Sampaio was sworn in for a second term as President on 9 March 2001.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2001-03-09 |title=Tomada de Posse de Jorge Sampaio |url=https://arquivos.rtp.pt/conteudos/tomada-de-posse-de-jorge-sampaio/| website= RTP Arquivos |access-date=2026-02-18 |language=pt-PT}}</ref>

==Background==
Jorge Sampaio was elected president, in January 1996, with nearly 54% of the votes.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2006-01-13 |title=Sampaio foi eleito Presidente da República há 10 anos  |url=https://www.rtp.pt/noticias/pais/sampaio-foi-eleito-presidente-da-republica-ha-10-anos_n23679 | website= RTP |access-date=2026-08-26 |language=pt-PT}}</ref> Sampaio's first term was marked by the calm relationship with prime minister [[António Guterres]], also from the Socialist Party (PS).<ref>{{Cite web |date=2021-09-10 |title=Os temas que marcaram a passagem de Jorge Sampaio por Belém |url=https://www.rtp.pt/noticias/politica/os-temas-que-marcaram-a-passagem-de-jorge-sampaio-por-belem_es1060892 | website= RTP |access-date=2026-08-26 |language=pt-PT}}</ref> Sampaio's term as head of state was also defined by his role in the struggle for [[Timor-Leste independence|East Timor's independence]], in which the president was an active voice against [[Indonesian occupation of East Timor|Indonesian occupation]] and demanded self-determination for the Timorese people,<ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-04-17 |title=Jorge Sampaio, o presidente que lutou por Timor e teve um papel marcante na libertação dos presos políticos no pós-25 de Abril |url=https://expresso.pt/podcasts/retratos-de-abril/2024-04-17-jorge-sampaio-o-presidente-que-lutou-por-timor-e-teve-um-papel-marcante-na-libertacao-dos-presos-politicos-no-pos-25-de-abril-a4bb7919 | website= Expresso |access-date=2026-08-26 |language=pt-PT}}</ref> the [[handover of Macau]] in 1999, where Sampaio was visibly moved,<ref>{{Cite web |date=2021-12-19 |title=Emoção no momento do adeus  |url=https://www.publico.pt/2019/12/19/mundo/reportagem/emocao-momento-adeus-1897165 | website= Público |access-date=2026-08-26 |language=pt-PT}}</ref> and the [[Expo '98]].

===Pre-campaign===

In the follow-up for the presidential election, and with Sampaio's victory a sure thing, divisions on the right-wing emerged regarding a common presidential candidate supported by both PSD and CDS–PP.<ref name=":0" /> The CDS–PP, at first, considered presenting a candidate of its own,<ref name=":CDS01" /> with 1991 candidate [[Basílio Horta]] announcing a candidacy, but dropping out after the summer of 2000.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2000-10-18 |title=Santana pede «serenidade» na análise da proposta popular |url=https://www.tsf.pt/arquivo/artigo/santana-pede-serenidade-na-analise-da-proposta-popular/754523 | website= TSF |access-date=2026-08-26 |language=pt-PT}}</ref> On the PSD, party leader [[José Manuel Durão Barroso]] invited former prime minister [[Francisco Pinto Balsemão]] to run against Sampaio, but Balsemão rejected.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2021-09-04 |title=As memórias de Balsemão, o político que escolheu o jornalismo |url=https://rr.pt/especial/politica/2021/09/04/as-memorias-de-balsemao-o-politico-que-escolheu-o-jornalismo/251973/ |access-date=2026-08-28 |website=Renascença |language=pt}}</ref> By then, the candidacy of former minister under Cavaco Silva, [[Joaquim Ferreira do Amaral]], which had announced his run in March 2000,<ref>{{Cite web |date=2000-03-31 |title=Candidatura de Ferreira do Amaral |url=https://arquivos.rtp.pt/conteudos/candidatura-de-ferreira-do-amaral/ | website= RTP |access-date=2026-08-26 |language=pt-PT}}</ref> became the sole one on the right-wing, gaining the support of the PSD and also of many CDS–PP members, with party leader [[Paulo Portas]] asking his electorate to "not vote for the socialist candidate".<ref>{{Cite web |date=2000-12-03 |title=Ferreira do Amaral diz que conta com apoio do CDS-PP  |url=https://www.publico.pt/2000/12/03/politica/noticia/ferreira-do-amaral-diz-que-conta-com-apoio-do-cdspp-2283 | website= Público |access-date=2026-08-26 |language=pt-PT}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2001-01-10 |title=Notáveis do PP apoiam Ferreira do Amaral |url=https://www.tsf.pt/arquivo/artigo/notaveis-do-pp-apoiam-ferreira-do-amaral/755437 | website= TSF |access-date=2026-08-26 |language=pt-PT}}</ref>

==Electoral system==
Any [[Portuguese nationality law|Portuguese citizen]] over 35 years old has the opportunity to run for president. In order to do so it is necessary to gather between 7,500 and 15,000 signatures and submit them to the [[Portuguese Constitutional Court]].

According to the [[Constitution of Portugal|Portuguese Constitution]], to be elected, a candidate needs a majority of votes. If no candidate gets this majority there will take place a second round between the two most voted candidates.

